## Significance evidence
* Interviews strictly first in 97.32% of replicates (one-sided bootstrap p ≈ 0.027).
* Head-to-head gaps (CI excludes 0 for all): vs Scenarios +11.34 pp [1.55, 21.13]; vs Prototyping +11.34 pp [2.05, 21.13]; vs Workshops +11.86 pp [3.61, 20.62]; vs Observation +17.01 pp [6.70, 27.32].
* Gap vs per-replicate next-best (ties possible at the 2nd place): mean +8.57 pp, CI [0.00, 16.50], gap<=0 in 2.68% of replicates.
* Denominator sensitivity: winner = Interviews for n=194 (50.00%), n=276 (35.14%), n=170 users of >=1 technique (57.06%); full ranking invariant.

## Non-reproducibility note for the cited 69.399% [69.062, 69.735]
* 69.399% of n=194 requires k=134.63 (impossible); the only exact fractions with n<=999 are 127/183, 254/366, ... (127/183 = 69.399%).
* Exhaustive search found no statistic in this file equal to 69.399% (all category shares over valid/all-276, all `quoted/not quoted` columns, ~100 single-column filters; best unrelated filter match 68.97% at k=20/n=29).
* Cited CI half-width 0.3365 pp implies SE≈0.17 pp → effective n≈20,000+; impossible for n≈194 (SE≈3.6 pp). Likely from a differently coded version of the survey data and/or a std/sqrt(B)-type CI formula.
* Conclusion: the identity of the technique (Interviews) IS reproducible and robust; the exact percentage and CI are not.
